tion/edn, per the contract note; (3) EXCLUDE /published/ from the SPA fallback so a missing manifest or artifact is a 404 rather than the shell — the reader defends itself by treating a text/html response as 'absent', but the host should not need it to; (4) fall back to /index.html otherwise, and if the host supports per-prefix fallbacks prefer /<locale>/* -> /<locale>/index.html so a document path arrives with the right lang in its first byte.\",\n    \"open-hax/services — build.output is now a single directory: dist/site. Anything in services#19's shape that references dist/, dist/cljs/, a repo-root index.html, or three merged docroots is stale.\",\n    \"open-hax/services — the deploy needs OPENHAX_GRAPHICS_ROOT and OPENHAX_MUSIC_ROOT if the galleries are to carry content in CI/containers; with neither set the build succeeds with empty galleries by design.\",\n    \"open-hax/knoxx (knoxx-publication-static-site-target) — the reader now asserts four things beyond the contract's minimum, all reader-side and all loud: :route/path must start with '/', :route/locale must be a keyword, :route/artifact must be a RELATIVE path under the content root (an absolute URL is rejected, not fetched), and :route/revision when present must be a concrete string. The website's fixtures in test/fixtures/published/ are the shape the adapter should be able to produce; if the adapter's real output diverges, one of the two is wrong.\",\n    \"open-hax/knoxx — the reader ignores and DROPS unknown manifest fields (they do not reach any downstream code), so a new field the website should honour needs a matching change in src/cljc/open_hax/website/law/published_manifest.cljc known-route-keys.\",\n    \"open-hax/foresight — the three :website/* invariants now have a real basis file: website AGENTS.md exists and states each one in its own section. The :invariant/basis entries pointing at 'open-hax/website AGENTS.md' can be given a revision once this branch lands.\"\n  ]\n}\n\nAUTHORITATIVE CONTRACT: /home/err/spaces/foresight/docs/notes/published-content-manifest-cross-repo-contract.md — read it. Any deviation from it is a defect, because two other repositories are being built against it right now.\n\nDO THIS:\n1. Read the actual diff: `git status` and `git diff` (and `git diff --stat`). Untracked files count — use `git status --porcelain` and read new files directly.\n2. RE-RUN their verification command yourself. Compare the real output to what they claimed. An overclaimed green is the most important thing you can find.\n3. For EVERY \"satisfied\" line in their `dod`, try to disprove it. Find the test or the code that actually establishes it. \"A test exists\" is not the same as \"the test asserts the thing\".\n4. Check the contract specifically:\n   - Is the manifest shape EXACTLY as specified (required fields, EDN, namespaced keys)?\n   - Does an ABSENT manifest render normally rather than erroring?\n   - Does a MISSING REQUIRED FIELD fail LOUDLY rather than blanking the page?\n   - Is an unknown field ignored?\n   - Is a selector keyword in a revision rejected?\n5. Look for the classic failure modes: silently swallowed errors, empty catch blocks, a fallback that hides a real failure, a test that passes because it asserts nothing, hardcoded values that should be parameters, and any claim of \"verified\" that was never actually run.\n6. Check the repo's own AGENTS.md rules were obeyed (zero warnings, namespace architecture, no new lint violations).\n\nReport findings most-severe first. If the work is genuinely sound, say so plainly — do not invent findings to seem useful. But if a DoD line is overclaimed, SAY WHICH ONE and quote the evidence that refutes it."}]

